  "worked_solution": "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-3}\\,\u27f9\\,y={z}^{-1/3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-3{y}^{-4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{3{z}^{4/3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3{z}^{4/3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{z}^{4/3}}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-\\frac{1}{{z}^{1/3}}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+3z\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3z\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x & =-3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x.$",
